<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Artuqid dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1102–<a href="/years/1409">1409</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Artuqids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Artuqid dynasty was a Turkoman dynasty that ruled parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Upper Mesopotamia</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> from the early 12th to the early 15th century. Founded by the sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artuq-ibn-ekseb">Artuq ibn Ekseb</a>, a Seljuk commander, it divided into branches at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasankeyf">Hasankeyf</a> and Amid, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardin">Mardin</a> and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harput">Harput</a>. The Artuqids fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a>, notably under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilghazi">Ilghazi</a> at the Field of Blood in 1119, and were later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/artuqid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Artuqid dynasty" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cenk847 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artuqidsmap.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 12 August 1121, at the Battle of Didgori, his army routed a large Muslim coalition led by the <b>Artuqid</b> ruler Ilghazi, and in 1122 he captured Tbilisi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1409 he took Mardin, the <b>Artuqids</b>' last stronghold; in 1410 he conquered Tabriz, had Shaykh Uways Jalayir executed and captured Baghdad, and Armenia fell under his control.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-qoyunlu">Qara Qoyunlu · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Zengi quickly became the chief Turkic potentate of northern Syria and Iraq, taking Aleppo from the squabbling <b>Artuqids</b> in 1128 and, after the siege of Edessa in 1144, capturing the County of Edessa from the Crusaders, a feat that made him a hero in the Muslim world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zengid-dynasty">Zengid dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Harput, a fortress near the upper Euphrates, came under <b>Artuqid</b> rule early in the 12th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/artuqids-of-harput">Artuqids of Harput</a></li><li><blockquote>The last <b>Artuqids</b> were displaced by the Qara Qoyunlu leader Qara Yusuf in 1409, and Mardin later passed to the Aq Qoyunlu and the Ottomans.</blockquote><a href="/state/artuqids-of-mardin">Artuqids of Mardin</a></li><li><blockquote>Its best-documented ruler is Toghan Arslan, nicknamed the Hunchback, who held Bitlis and Erzen in the early 12th century, allied with the <b>Artuqid</b> Ilghazi against the Georgians and the Crusaders, and fought his neighbours over Mayyafariqin.</blockquote><a href="/state/dilmacogullari">Dilmaçoğulları</a></li></ul></section>
